## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates understanding of the Trie (prefix tree) data structure, covering insert, delete, and prefix-query operations with proper edge case handling.

## Key Concepts

- **Trie node structure**: Each node stores 26 child pointers, an end-of-word flag, and a pass-through counter for efficient prefix queries
- **Safe deletion**: Decrement counts along the path and only unmark end-of-word when the word actually exists
- **Prefix query**: Return the count at the prefix's last node in O(length of prefix) time
- **Duplicate handling**: Adding the same word twice increments the counter twice; deleting decrements twice, effectively removing one occurrence

## Code Example

```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Array(26).fill(null);
    this.isEndOfWord = false;
    this.count = 0;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  add(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      const index = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.children[index]) {
        node.children[index] = new TrieNode();
      }
      node = node.children[index];
      node.count++;
    }
    node.isEndOfWord = true;
  }

  delete(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      const index = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.children[index]) return;
      node = node.children[index];
      node.count--;
    }
    node.isEndOfWord = false;
  }

  countPrefix(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      const index = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.children[index]) return 0;
      node = node.children[index];
    }
    return node.count;
  }
}
```

## Why This Question Matters

This problem evaluates a candidate's ability to extend standard grid DP problems with additional state dimensions. Modeling exact-turn constraints is essential in path-planning scenarios such as robot navigation, vehicle routing, and game movement where turn penalties or restrictions exist.

## Core Concepts

- **State Design**: Extending traditional grid DP with turn count and last direction dimensions
- **Transition Logic**: Increment turn count when direction changes between moves
- **Space Optimization**: Using row-wise or 2-layer rolling arrays to reduce memory from O(n*m*K) to O(m*K)
- **Edge Cases**: Handling obstacles, impossible turn requirements, and boundary conditions

## Reference Implementation

```javascript
function maxPathSumWithTurns(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const INF = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: m }, () =>
      Array.from({ length: K + 1 }, () => [INF, INF])
    )
  );

  dp[0][0][0][0] = dp[0][0][0][1] = grid[0][0];

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      if (grid[i][j] === -1) continue;
      for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
        if (j + 1 < m && grid[i][j+1] !== -1) {
          const newTurn = t + (i > 0 ? 1 : 0);
          if (newTurn <= K) {
            dp[i][j+1][newTurn][0] = Math.max(
              dp[i][j+1][newTurn][0],
              dp[i][j][t][1] + grid[i][j+1]
            );
          }
          dp[i][j+1][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i][j+1][t][0], dp[i][j][t][0] + grid[i][j+1]);
        }
        if (i + 1 < n && grid[i+1][j] !== -1) {
          const newTurn = t + (j > 0 ? 1 : 0);
          if (newTurn <= K) {
            dp[i+1][j][newTurn][1] = Math.max(
              dp[i+1][j][newTurn][1],
              dp[i][j][t][0] + grid[i+1][j]
            );
          }
          dp[i+1][j][t][1] = Math.max(dp[i+1][j][t][1], dp[i][j][t][1] + grid[i+1][j]);
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const result = Math.max(...dp[n-1][m-1].flat());
  return result === INF ? -1 : result;
}
